> Where do you get the idea that zo'e is a constant? As I understand it, {m=
i=20
> viska ci cakcinki} is equally true if I see three Japanese beetles (of wh=
ich=20
> there are many on the blackberry plants) or if I see a cocuyo, a scarab, =
and a=20
> weevil.=20

xorxes, at least, intended for zo'e to be a constant [1]. zo'e=20
(especially explicit zo'e) as a variable would be problematic for=20
negation [2].

[1] https://mw.lojban.org/papri/BPFK_Section:_gadri#Notes_2
[2] http://mail.lojban.org/lojban-list/msg47182.html

> I=E2=80=99m pretty sure this is not how all this works. If a place isn=E2=
=80=99t invoked,
> it doesn=E2=80=99t participate in the quantification; therefore, =C2=ABso=
=E2=80=99i cakcinki cu
> xabju lo purdi=C2=BB has *unary* =C2=ABcakcinki=C2=BB, so the correct mea=
ning =E2=80=93 =E2=80=98there are
> many beetles living in the garden.=E2=80=99

In practice it does seem like unfilled places don't exist, although if=20
you ask people they'll usually say that there is something filling them,=20
most often zo'e [3-5], hence my slightly tongue-in-cheek outrage.

[3] https://mw.lojban.org/papri/la_karda#Elliptical_words
[4] https://lojban.github.io/cll/7/7/
[5] https://mw.lojban.org/papri/Lojban_Wave_Lessons/2

Some will say unfilled places are zi'o, which is blessed with at least=20
two competing meanings, or that they are ambiguous between zo'e and zi'o.

zi'o1: remove the place, creating a new predicate that has nothing do to=20
with the removed place
zi'o2: remove the place on the surface by substituting {be su'oi da}

zi'o2 is, imo, only marginally better than zo'e, as it still takes only=20
one value when in a LO phrase.

zi'o1 is nice, but feels pragmatically odd in that when explicit, it's=20
supposed to make an utterance less specific by adding words to it, which=20
might give a weak implicature that {broda zi'o .e no da}. Though this is=20
just baggage from zo'e officially being default I think -- implicit=20
zi'o1 should be free of it.

>=20
> la tersmu agrees:
>   purdi(c0); (so'i)x1:(cakcinki(_)). zvati(x1,c0)

(I wouldn't think tersmu has much to say here as it doesn't have a=20
dictionary of predicates' arities)

